Lightweight Screen Recorder

A small floating icon that sits on top of your screen. Click it to start/stop video recording, take a screenshot, or pick your save folder. Files save automatically with timestamped names — no dialogs to click through.

No audio, no bloat: just mss (screen capture) + opencv-python (video encode) + Tkinter (the icon UI, built into Python).

SETUP: 
======================================================================
bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
========================================================================
Run
======================================================================
bash
python screen_recorder.py
======================================================================

A small dark circle icon will appear near the top-right of your screen.

Click it → opens a small menu (Start/Stop Recording, Screenshot, Choose Save Folder, Open Save Folder, Quit)
Drag it → moves it anywhere on screen
First run saves to ~/Videos/ScreenCapture by default; pick a different folder from the menu and it is remembered for next time.

Recordings are named Recording_2026-08-09_14-30-05.mp4, screenshots Screenshot_2026-08-09_14-30-05.png.

Tuning

At the top of screen_recorder.py:

python
FPS = 12      # lower = smaller files, less CPU. 12-15 is a good default.
SCALE = 1.0   # e.g. 0.75 to record at 75% resolution

PLATFORM NOTES:

Windows / macOS — works out of the box.
macOS — first run, grant "Screen Recording" permission to your Terminal/Python app in System Settings → Privacy & Security → Screen Recording, or captures come out black.
Linux — capture works on X11. Plain Wayland sessions block this kind of screen capture for security reasons — run under X11/XWayland.

KNOWN LIMITATION:

Because the icon is genuinely on your screen, it will appear in the corner of your video recordings (screenshots hide it automatically first). Drag it somewhere less distracting before recording, or just crop it out later if it bothers you.
